2025-05-01 02:19:54 - Thread-793 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns & why they matter  
• `pitcherId` ‑ unique identifier of the pitcher; used to link the pitch to a player  
• `pitchSpeed` ‑ value we want to maximise; must be > 0 (non‑zero)  
• `pitcherFirstName`, `pitcherLastName` ‑ to build the pitcher’s full name for the result  
• `homeTeamId`, `awayTeamId` ‑ to decide which club the pitcher represents in that row  
• `homeFielder1 … homeFielder12`, `awayFielder1 … awayFielder12` ‑ lists of player‑IDs that took the field; we confirm the `pitcherId` really appears in the correct list for that game  
• `gameId` – handy join key if we later need to reach other tables  
• Tables:  
  – `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_wide`  (regular season)  
  – `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_post_wide` (post‑season)  

Typical conditions  
• `pitchSpeed IS NOT NULL AND pitchSpeed > 0` – keep only valid, non‑zero speeds  
• `pitcherId IN (…)` – ensure the pitcherId is present in the appropriate home/away fielder list  
• Use `CASE` to assign the correct club id/name depending on which list the pitcherId occurs in.

Below are ten exploratory → increasingly complex queries (all `SELECT`, each limited to 20 rows) that build up to the final answer.  
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

```sql
--Description: 1. Peek at a handful of pitchSpeed values in regular‑season data
SELECT gameId, pitchSpeed, pitcherId
FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_wide`
WHERE pitchSpeed IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2. Show distinct non‑zero pitch speeds together with pitcher names (regular season)
SELECT DISTINCT pitchSpeed, pitcherFirstName, pitcherLastName
FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_wide`
WHERE pitchSpeed > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3. Sample post‑season rows with pitcherId and pitchSpeed
SELECT gameId, pitchSpeed, pitcherId, pitcherFirstName, pitcherLastName
FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_post_wide`
WHERE pitchSpeed > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4. Verify that a pitcher really appears in a HOME fielder list (regular season example)
SELECT gameId, pitcherId, homeTeamId
FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_wide`
WHERE pitchSpeed > 0
  AND pitcherId IN (homeFielder1, homeFielder2, homeFielder3,
                    homeFielder4, homeFielder5, homeFielder6,
                    homeFielder7, homeFielder8, homeFielder9,
                    homeFielder10, homeFielder11, homeFielder12)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5. Same idea for an AWAY fielder confirmation (post‑season example)
SELECT gameId, pitcherId, awayTeamId
FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_post_wide`
WHERE pitchSpeed > 0
  AND pitcherId IN (awayFielder1, awayFielder2, awayFielder3,
                    awayFielder4, awayFielder5, awayFielder6,
                    awayFielder7, awayFielder8, awayFielder9,
                    awayFielder10, awayFielder11, awayFielder12)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6. Combine regular + post‑season with UNION ALL and keep a few rows
SELECT gameId, seasonType, pitchSpeed, pitcherId
FROM (
  SELECT gameId, seasonType, pitchSpeed, pitcherId
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_wide`
  UNION ALL
  SELECT gameId, seasonType, pitchSpeed, pitcherId
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_post_wide`
)
WHERE pitchSpeed > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 7. Determine the maximum pitchSpeed per pitcher (all seasons)
SELECT pitcherId,
       MAX(pitchSpeed) AS max_pitch_speed
FROM (
  SELECT pitcherId, pitchSpeed
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_wide`
  UNION ALL
  SELECT pitcherId, pitchSpeed
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_post_wide`
)
WHERE pitchSpeed > 0
GROUP BY pitcherId
ORDER BY max_pitch_speed DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 8. Attach pitcher names to the previous max‑speed list
SELECT p.pitcherId,
       ANY_VALUE(p.pitcherFirstName) AS first_name,
       ANY_VALUE(p.pitcherLastName)  AS last_name,
       MAX(p.pitchSpeed)             AS max_pitch_speed
FROM (
  SELECT pitcherId, pitcherFirstName, pitcherLastName, pitchSpeed
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_wide`
  UNION ALL
  SELECT pitcherId, pitcherFirstName, pitcherLastName, pitchSpeed
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_post_wide`
) AS p
WHERE p.pitchSpeed > 0
GROUP BY p.pitcherId
ORDER BY max_pitch_speed DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 9. For each (team, pitcher) pair verify the pitcher is on that team’s fielder list
SELECT DISTINCT
       CASE
         WHEN pitcherId IN (homeFielder1, homeFielder2, homeFielder3, homeFielder4,
                            homeFielder5, homeFielder6, homeFielder7, homeFielder8,
                            homeFielder9, homeFielder10, homeFielder11, homeFielder12)
              THEN homeTeamId
         WHEN pitcherId IN (awayFielder1, awayFielder2, awayFielder3, awayFielder4,
                            awayFielder5, awayFielder6, awayFielder7, awayFielder8,
                            awayFielder9, awayFielder10, awayFielder11, awayFielder12)
              THEN awayTeamId
       END AS confirmed_team_id,
       pitcherId,
       pitchSpeed
FROM (
  SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_wide`
  UNION ALL
  SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_post_wide`
)
WHERE pitchSpeed > 0
  AND (
        pitcherId IN (homeFielder1, homeFielder2, homeFielder3, homeFielder4,
                      homeFielder5, homeFielder6, homeFielder7, homeFielder8,
                      homeFielder9, homeFielder10, homeFielder11, homeFielder12)
        OR
        pitcherId IN (awayFielder1, awayFielder2, awayFielder3, awayFielder4,
                      awayFielder5, awayFielder6, awayFielder7, awayFielder8,
                      awayFielder9, awayFielder10, awayFielder11, awayFielder12)
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 10. FINAL – highest non‑zero pitch speed for each team with the pitcher’s full name (regular + post‑season)
SELECT
  team_id,
  MAX(pitch_speed) AS max_valid_pitch_speed,
  ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT CONCAT(pitcherFirstName, ' ', pitcherLastName)
            ORDER BY pitch_speed DESC LIMIT 1)[OFFSET(0)] AS pitcher_full_name
FROM (
  SELECT
    CASE
      WHEN pitcherId IN (homeFielder1, homeFielder2, homeFielder3, homeFielder4,
                         homeFielder5, homeFielder6, homeFielder7, homeFielder8,
                         homeFielder9, homeFielder10, homeFielder11, homeFielder12)
           THEN homeTeamId
      WHEN pitcherId IN (awayFielder1, awayFielder2, awayFielder3, awayFielder4,
                         awayFielder5, awayFielder6, awayFielder7, awayFielder8,
                         awayFielder9, awayFielder10, awayFielder11, awayFielder12)
           THEN awayTeamId
      ELSE NULL
    END AS team_id,
    pitchSpeed              AS pitch_speed,
    pitcherFirstName,
    pitcherLastName,
    pitcherId
  FROM (
    SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_wide`
    UNION ALL
    SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.baseball.games_post_wide`
  )
  WHERE pitchSpeed > 0
        AND (
              pitcherId IN (homeFielder1, homeFielder2, homeFielder3, homeFielder4,
                            homeFielder5, homeFielder6, homeFielder7, homeFielder8,
                            homeFielder9, homeFielder10, homeFielder11, homeFielder12)
              OR
              pitcherId IN (awayFielder1, awayFielder2, awayFielder3, awayFielder4,
                            awayFielder5, awayFielder6, awayFielder7, awayFielder8,
                            awayFielder9, awayFielder10, awayFielder11, awayFielder12)
            )
)
GROUP BY team_id
ORDER BY max_valid_pitch_speed DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

Each successive query digs a bit deeper—from raw sampling of pitch speeds, to validation of pitcher/team pairing, and finally to the consolidated “fastest pitcher per team” result that spans both the regular season and post‑season.
